      "In January 1918 seven ministers representing six churches meet at Kingsville, Texas, to begin a Latin convention among Spanish-speaking Assemblies of God congregations. It was organized by Isabel Flores, a Mexican-American pastor, and Henry C. Ball, an Anglo missionary who had started a small Pentecostal mission among refugees of the Mexican Civil War at Ricardo, Texas. A second convention followed in November. The body became the Latin American District Council, chartered in 1930 with Ball as first superintendent. Historian Gastón Espinosa notes that paternalism toward Mexican leaders later brought a schism.",
