      "The Christian Reformed Church opens Rehoboth Mission School in 1903 for Navajo and Zuni children on the former Smith's Ranch near Gallup, New Mexico. The Dutch-descended missionaries named the settlement Rehoboth, 'plenty of room', from Genesis 26:22. It opened with six Navajo children, and in its early boarding years pupils were forbidden to speak Navajo. Control passed in the 1970s to a board of mostly Native parents, the school became Rehoboth Christian School in 1973, and Navajo language courses were added in 2002. A Calvin history dates the mission church to 1899, so the start varies by source.",
