      "Alfred Longley Riggs, the son of the missionaries Stephen R. and Mary Riggs, founds the Santee Normal Training School in Nebraska in 1870 to train the Santee Sioux. The Nebraska State Historical Society calls him a Congregational missionary, while the Presbyterian Historical Society places the school within the Riggs family's Dakota mission. Riggs' official report held Dakota to be indispensable to the best instruction, and much of the school's printed material is in the Dakota language. Riggs ran it until his death in 1916, and his son Frederick led it until 1933.",
